==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gmh|de}} {{term|ziehen|lang=gmh}}, from {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|ziohan|lang=goh}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{recons|teuhaną|lang=gem-pro}}. Cognate with Gothic {{term|𐍄𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽|tr=tiuhan|lang=got}}, Middle Dutch {{term|tien|tīen|lang=dum}}, Old English {{term|teon|tēon|lang=ang}}, Old Norse {{term|toga|lang=non}}, West Frisian {{term|tsjen|lang=fy}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|De-ziehen.ogg|Audio}}
{{rfp|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-strong|class=2|zieht|zog|gezogen|past subjunctive=zöge|auxiliary=both}}

# {{transitive|or|intransitive|auxiliary: “[[haben]]”}} to [[pull]] (e.g., a door handle); to [[drag]]
# {{transitive|auxiliary: “[[haben]]”|lang=de}} to [[draw]] (e.g. a weapon); to [[extract]]; to [[puff]]
# {{transitive|auxiliary: “[[haben]]”|lang=de}} to [[draw]] (a conclusion, lesson, etc.)
#* '''2010''', ''{{w|Der Spiegel}}'', issue [http://www.spiegel.de/spiegel/print/index-2010-25.html 25/2010], page 77:
#*:Es gilt deshalb, die richtigen Lehren aus der Krise zu '''ziehen''', aus den Fehlern der Vergangenheit zu lernen, um die Zukunft zu sichern.
#*::Therefore it is necessary to draw the right lessons from the crisis, to learn from the mistakes of the past for securing the future.
# {{impersonal|intransitive|lang=de}} to [[be]] [[drafty]]
#: ''Es '''zieht'''.'' — “It’s drafty.”
# {{intransitive|auxiliary: “[[sein]]”}} to [[move]]; to [[migrate]]
#: ''Ich '''ziehe''' nach Hamburg, aber mein Bruder '''zieht''' in eine andere Stadt.''
# {{intransitive|auxiliary: “[[sein]]”}} to [[roam]]
# {{reflexive|auxiliary: “[[haben]]”}} to [[stretch]]; to [[warp]]

